  "content": {
    "id": "v2-842d0-p42-practice-slot-2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Use long division to show that $P(x)=x^3+x^2-14x-24$ can be divided by $x+2$ with no remainder and hence express $P(x)$ as the product of three linear factors.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Quotient $x^2-x-12$; remainder $0$.\n\n$P(x)=(x-4)(x+3)(x+2)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{array}{r|l}&x^2-x-12\\\\\\hline x+2&x^3+x^2-14x-24\\\\&\\underline{-(x^3+2x^2)}\\\\&-x^2-14x-24\\\\&\\underline{-(-x^2-2x)}\\\\&-12x-24\\\\&\\underline{-(-12x-24)}\\\\&0\\end{array}$$\nThe remainder is $0$, so $P(x)$ is divisible by $x+2$.\nPSF for $x^2-x-12$: Product $-12$, Sum $-1$, Factors $-4$ and $3$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^2-x-12&=(x-4)(x+3)\\\\P(x)&=(x+2)(x^2-x-12)\\\\P(x)&=(x-4)(x+3)(x+2).\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
